# ONE GPC, THREE DISPLAYS -- the CPU-BOUND benchmark, which is its whole point.
#
#   cd discretePanel
#   python3 simulatePASS.py --gpcs 1 --crts 3 \
#       --tape ~/workspace/pass-run/OI340700-v44boot.mmv \
#       --script examples/bench-1gpc.script --duration 600 --no-manager
#
# THIS IS NOT A CREW PROCEDURE.  It is the measuring stick for optimisation
# work, kept here because the alternatives do not measure anything:
#
#   * The headless regression gate (~/workspace/pass-run/headless-gpcmem.sh)
#     runs with in-process mass memory and DEUs and PACES ITSELF TO REAL TIME
#     -- measured, rate 1.16 even on a 2011 i7-3930K.  It therefore does the
#     same work however fast the host is and cannot show a change of a few per
#     cent.  It is a functional test, not a benchmark (ledger #160).
#
#   * A four-GPC run costs about forty minutes a leg and adds barrier
#     interaction between the computers, which muddies attribution.
#
#   One GPC driving networked displays sits PINNED at ~98% of a core at about
#   a fifth of real time, so every cycle saved becomes simulated time gained
#   and the rate moves.  It does not exercise the inter-GPC discrete traffic;
#   that needs peers.
#
# HOW TO READ THE RESULT.  Do NOT set YAGPC_PACETRACE to get the rate: run.c
# takes two wall-clock readings per exec batch and two per bus service when
# that trace is on, and on a host whose kernel has fallen back to the hpet
# clocksource each reading is a syscall.  Switching on the trace that reports
# the rate is what depresses it -- measured, it compressed a real 4.7%
# improvement to 1.5%.  Use the step count printed at shutdown instead, over
# measured wall time: same tape, same script, same phase, so steps per second
# compares two builds honestly.
#
# AND CHECK THE RUN WAS VALID FIRST.  If panel.log holds "WAIT TIMED OUT" the
# crew script stopped, the GPC sat in a wait state for the rest of the run,
# and the step count is meaningless -- that is how a good build once reported
# 40612 steps/s against 701144 (ledger #167).
#
# Reference figures on a 2011 i7-3930K, 600 s, uninstrumented, after the
# display-buffer fix brought run-to-run spread to 0.2%:
#     340906 steps/s   commit 45cf0856f
#     698496 steps/s   commit 24ac489a0  (the attend() clock-read fix)

+0     gpc 1
+0.01  mode HALT
+0.02  idppower 1 on
+0.01  idppower 2 on
+0.01  script ipl-one-gpc.script gpc=1 crt=1 idp=1 kb=KB1
